<section class="tale-hero" id="top"><div class="tale-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/stories.html">Stories</a><span>/</span><a href="/deities.html#devi">The Goddess</a></nav><p class="eyebrow">THE DEVI MAHATMYA / THE FIRST EPISODE</p><h1>The universe slept.<br><em>The Goddess stirred awakening.</em></h1><p class="tale-deck">On the limitless waters, Vishnu rests in cosmic sleep while Madhu and Kaitabha threaten Brahma. The power that veils awareness becomes the power that restores it.</p><div class="tale-meta"><span>Devi Mahatmya · Chapter 1</span><span>About {{ storyCatalog.mahamaya.minutes }} min read</span><a href="/sources.html#mahamaya-madhu-kaitabha">View sources</a></div><a class="button" href="#forest">Begin the story</a></div><figure class="tale-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/mahamaya-madhu-kaitabha-hero.webp" alt="Mahamaya rising as violet-gold radiance above sleeping Vishnu on Shesha while four-headed Brahma prays from the lotus and Madhu and Kaitabha approach across the cosmic waters" width="1536" height="1024"><figcaption>MAHAMAYA AND THE COSMIC SLEEP</figcaption></figure></section>
<div class="tale-introduction"><p>From the <strong>Markandeya Purana · Devi Mahatmya</strong>.</p></div>
<section class="story-characters" aria-labelledby="characters-title"><p class="eyebrow">MEET THE CHARACTERS</p><h2 id="characters-title">Figures in this story</h2><dl><div><dt>Mahamaya</dt><dd>The eternal Goddess described as cosmic sleep, the power through which worlds appear, and the source of both delusion and liberating knowledge.</dd></div><div><dt>Vishnu</dt><dd>Lord of preservation, resting upon Shesha at the end of a cosmic age until Mahamaya withdraws and he awakens.</dd></div><div><dt>Brahma</dt><dd>The creator seated upon the lotus from Vishnu, who praises the Goddess when Madhu and Kaitabha threaten him.</dd></div><div><dt>Madhu and Kaitabha</dt><dd>Two powerful asuras who arise upon the primordial waters, challenge Brahma and later confront Vishnu.</dd></div><div><dt>Medhas</dt><dd>The sage who tells the episode to King Suratha and the merchant Samadhi in response to their question about attachment.</dd></div></dl></section>
<div class="tale-layout"><aside class="tale-contents"><p class="eyebrow">IN THIS STORY</p><nav aria-label="Story chapters"><a href="#forest"><span>01</span>Two questions in the forest</a><a href="#mahamaya"><span>02</span>The power that binds and releases</a><a href="#waters"><span>03</span>The ocean before awakening</a><a href="#hymn"><span>04</span>Brahma praises the Goddess</a><a href="#awakening"><span>05</span>Mahamaya withdraws</a><a href="#battle"><span>06</span>The struggle on the waters</a><a href="#boon"><span>07</span>Pride becomes an opening</a><a href="#answer"><span>08</span>The answer within the story</a><a href="#reading-note">Text &amp; interpretation</a><a href="/sources.html#mahamaya-madhu-kaitabha">Sources &amp; reading notes</a></nav><a class="tale-back" href="/stories.html">Return to the collection</a></aside><article class="tale-body">{% include "reader-tools.njk" %}
<section class="tale-chapter" id="forest"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>01</span><h2>Two questions in the forest</h2></div><p>King Suratha has lost his kingdom. Rival rulers defeated him, and ministers he trusted took his treasury and army. Though he has reached the quiet hermitage of the sage Medhas, his mind keeps returning to the city, the people and even the royal elephant now held by his enemies.</p><p>Nearby he meets Samadhi, a merchant driven from home by a family intent upon his wealth. Samadhi knows how they treated him, yet still wonders whether they are safe and living well.</p><p>Together they ask Medhas why knowledge has not freed them from attachment. They can see the fault in what troubles them, but their minds continue to circle it. The sage answers with the first episode of the Devi Mahatmya.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="mahamaya"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>02</span><h2>The power that binds and releases</h2></div><p>Medhas begins with a world wider than human reasoning. Creatures care for their young even when hungry themselves; people act from affection, expectation and self-interest in ways that knowledge alone does not dissolve. Awareness of a pattern is not always enough to escape it.</p><p>This power, he says, is Mahamaya. She can draw even the knowing mind into delusion. Yet she is not merely a mistake to be discarded. She is eternal, embodied as the world, the power through which the moving and unmoving universe appears. When gracious, she is also supreme knowledge and the giver of liberation.</p><p>To show how one power can veil and awaken, bind and release, Medhas turns to the end of an earlier cosmic age.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="waters"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>03</span><h2>The ocean before awakening</h2></div><p>The universe has become a single expanse of water. Vishnu rests upon Shesha in the deep sleep of contemplation. From him rises a lotus, and upon that lotus sits Brahma.</p><p>In this vast stillness, two asuras arise: Madhu and Kaitabha. Powerful and fierce, they move toward Brahma with the intention of destroying him. Brahma sees the danger—but he also sees that Vishnu remains asleep.</p><p>The sleep dwelling in Vishnu is not described as ordinary exhaustion. It is the presence of the Goddess, the cosmic power called Yoganidra and Mahamaya. Brahma therefore directs his appeal to her.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="hymn"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>04</span><h2>Brahma praises the Goddess</h2></div><p>Brahma praises Mahamaya as the support of the universe, the power within creation, preservation and withdrawal. His hymn moves across apparent opposites: she is memory and delusion, good fortune and the terrible night, gentle beyond gentleness and formidable in defence.</p><p>The prayer does not ask her to replace Vishnu in the coming battle. It asks her to release him from sleep, awaken his intelligence and turn her power upon the two asuras. The roles remain distinct, yet neither is independent of the Goddess whose presence has held even the preserver of the worlds in stillness.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="awakening"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>05</span><h2>Mahamaya withdraws</h2></div><p>Answering Brahma’s praise, Mahamaya issues from Vishnu’s eyes, mouth, breath, arms, heart and chest. She stands revealed before Brahma as the dark and radiant power that had filled Vishnu’s contemplation.</p><p>Released from her cosmic sleep, Vishnu rises upon the ocean. He sees Madhu and Kaitabha ready to attack Brahma. Rest has become awareness; awareness becomes action.</p><p>This is the centre of the episode’s title and theology: Mahamaya does not simply wake someone who happens to be sleeping. The power that covered consciousness deliberately withdraws, making recognition and response possible.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="battle"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>06</span><h2>The struggle on the waters</h2></div><p>Vishnu confronts the two asuras upon the measureless ocean. The text expands the battle to a cosmic duration, conveying not a brief exchange but opponents whose strength and confidence seem inexhaustible.</p><figure class="tale-inline story-landscape"><img src="/assets/images/vishnu-madhu-kaitabha.webp" alt="Four-armed Vishnu with conch, discus, mace and lotus confronting the distinct warriors Madhu and Kaitabha on the primordial ocean beneath the vast indigo-gold presence of Mahamaya" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>VISHNU CONFRONTS MADHU AND KAITABHA</figcaption></figure><p>Vishnu fights, but Mahamaya remains essential to what follows. The pair become intoxicated with their own power. Unable to read the danger in front of them, they mistake endurance for mastery.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="boon"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>07</span><h2>Pride becomes an opening</h2></div><p>Deluded by Mahamaya, Madhu and Kaitabha speak as though they are the victors. They invite Vishnu to choose a boon from them.</p><p>Vishnu accepts the opening hidden inside their pride: let his boon be that the two may be defeated by him. Realising the turn too late, they look upon a world covered entirely by water and add a condition—that their end must come where the earth is not submerged.</p><p>Vishnu agrees. Meeting the condition they have named, he ends the struggle with his discus. The narrative remains exact about agency: Mahamaya awakens and deludes; Vishnu recognises the opening and completes the defeat.</p></section>
<section class="tale-chapter" id="answer"><div class="tale-chapter-heading"><span>08</span><h2>The answer within the story</h2></div><p>Medhas has answered Suratha and Samadhi through narrative rather than definition alone. Mahamaya can hold Vishnu in cosmic sleep and turn the confidence of mighty asuras into delusion. No one should imagine that knowing a fact makes the mind entirely independent of her power.</p><p>Yet the story does not end in helplessness. The same Goddess who veils also reveals. Brahma recognises whom to address; Mahamaya withdraws; Vishnu awakens and acts. The first charita therefore introduces a theme that will continue through the Devi Mahatmya: divine power is present not only in what appears, but in the possibility of seeing differently.</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reflection"><p class="eyebrow">EDITORIAL REFLECTION / PAUSE &amp; REFLECT</p><h2>What changes when awareness<br><em>truly wakes?</em></h2><p>Suratha and Samadhi already understand the facts of their loss, but their minds remain caught. Where in your own life might more information be less important than a different quality of attention?</p></section>
<section class="tale-reflection" id="reading-note"><p class="eyebrow">TEXT &amp; INTERPRETATION</p><h2>The Goddess awakens.<br><em>Vishnu completes the battle.</em></h2><p>This page follows Canto 81 of the supplied F. E. Pargiter translation of the Markandeya Purana, corresponding to Devi Mahatmya chapter 1. It retains the Suratha–Samadhi frame, Medhas’s teaching, Brahma’s hymn, Mahamaya’s withdrawal, Vishnu’s awakening and the asuras’ self-defeating boon.</p><p>“Great Illusion” is the supplied translation’s frequent rendering of Mahamaya, but the same passage also calls her supreme knowledge, the eternal cause of liberation and the power embodied as the universe. This reader therefore does not reduce maya to a simple synonym for falsehood.</p><p>The combat is condensed and non-graphic. The paintings are contemporary devotional interpretations: the faces, armour, ocean and visual form of Mahamaya are imagined, not historical reconstructions or literal diagrams of the text.</p><a href="/sources.html#mahamaya-madhu-kaitabha">Read the verse references and supplied-edition notes</a></section>
